{
  "slug": "mandukya-karika",
  "title": "Gaudapada's Mandukya Karika",
  "originalTitle": "माण्डूक्यकारिका",
  "transliteration": "Māṇḍūkyakārikā",
  "altTitles": [
    "Gauḍapādakārikā",
    "Agamasastra",
    "Mandukya Karika"
  ],
  "tradition": "hinduism",
  "school": "vedanta",
  "genre": "Philosophy",
  "textType": "Philosophical verses",
  "category": "Vedanta",
  "subcategory": "Advaita Vedanta",
  "originalLanguage": "sa",
  "dateStart": 400,
  "dateEnd": 700,
  "dateDisplay": "c. 6th century CE (date unsettled)",
  "authorshipStatus": "traditional",
  "author": "gaudapada",
  "attributionNote": "The four books of verses on the Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad ascribed to Gauḍapāda, the teacher of Śaṅkara's teacher's teacher. GRETIL's e-text is taken from the commented edition, where the names of the verses come from the text as the tradition transmitted it; it was input by members of the Sansknet project.",
  "description": "The Kārikā is the earliest systematic exposition of Advaita: it reads the four states of the self in the Māṇḍūkya as the four quarters of om and argues that the world of experience is, like a dream and like a rope seen as a snake, a construction that the knowledge of the unborn, non-dual self dissolves. Its second book works through illusion and causality, its third expounds non-dualism, and its last, the alātaśānti, argues with the firebrand whose moving spark traces a circle that exists nowhere.",
  "historicalContext": "The Kārikā is the hinge of the Advaita tradition: the first Advaita text to argue its case philosophically, it was commented on by Śaṅkara and, in the school's teaching, is bound to the Upaniṣad it interprets. Its doctrine of non-origination was answered by the Buddhist philosophers and by Rāmānuja.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete: all four books of the Kārikā, 215 verses in four chapters, streamed from GRETIL at read time. Each passage is one verse, addressed as book.verse.",
